FEATURE: component-module
DATE: 2026-09-17
SCOPE: add vUX-component.js — a consumer's own reusable component — on a shared
       template-instancing engine, and move DataView onto the same engine
FOLLOWS: changelog/component-teardown

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
AFFECTED PROJECTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

vUX library (this repository) — the only project affected. No build step, no
dependency graph; changes take effect on the next page load.

Consumer-visible surface:
  - vUX-component.js          new module: Component + Instance
  - src/componentEngine.js    new internal file, shared by Component and DataView
  - vUX-dataView.js           reimplemented over the engine; public surface unchanged
  - src/helpers.js            module registry entry, so vUxModules lists it
  - doc/component.md          new guide
  - examples/component/       new example page

Additive. No member was renamed or removed and no existing call signature changed.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT WAS BUILT, AND WHY IN THIS SHAPE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Before this, a vUX consumer had sixteen ready-made components for markup they had
already written, and nothing at all for the reusable pieces of their own interface.
The moment they wanted their own card repeated forty times they were back to
$$.ce() and string concatenation. That is the wall at which people stop using vUX
for the application and reach for a framework.

The shape chosen is template instancing, not Web Components:

  - Shadow DOM was rejected outright. $$.ss and $$.sa are document.querySelector
    and stop at a shadow boundary, and linkStyleSheet()/attachStyleSheet() both
    append to document.head, where styles cannot reach inside one. vUX's contract
    is global CSS plus consumer-supplied class names; Shadow DOM's contract is no
    global CSS. Adopting one means abandoning the other.

  - A customElements.define() wrapper was rejected as well. Over the four lines the
    browser already gives you it would have to add reactive properties and
    re-rendering, which is Lit. The guide documents the six-line recipe for
    wrapping a Component in a custom element instead, so the tag stays the
    consumer's and vUX does not own that surface.

  - What was built is the thing vUX already had half of: DataView's proven
    'mark a template, detach it, clone per item, bind fields by key' machinery,
    generalised from table rows to any fragment.

The scope is fixed and stated in the guide so it does not get re-argued: instancing,
field/attribute/event/class/show bindings, refs, keyed lists, nesting and teardown.
No reactivity, no observables, no computed properties, no diffing, no scheduler, no
data-v-if, no data-v-for. Every binding value is a plain field name, never an
expression — which is the actual line between a binding layer and a template
language, and it is enforced by there being no evaluator in the code at all.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY IT FOLLOWED THE TEARDOWN WORK
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

onDestroy is the seam at which a consumer releases the vUX components nested inside
their own component. Until the component-teardown feature, destroy() existed on two
of sixteen components, so that callback would have had almost nothing to call. The
example page's section 04 demonstrates the pair working together.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE DATAVIEW REFACTOR
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

sync() and DataView's reconcile() are the same function, so they are now one
function. DataView keeps its whole public surface and every error string verbatim;
the engine takes an 'itemNoun' purely so its keying errors keep saying 'row object'
for DataView.

Consumers of DataView gain the wider binding set in rows for free: previously only
data-v-field worked, now data-v-bind, data-v-on, data-v-ref, data-v-show and
data-v-class do too, with config.handlers and config.formatters added to drive them.

One latent bug surfaced and was fixed: DataView.destroy() never cleared
'initialized', which made its own 'This DataView has been destroyed' guard
unreachable — re-initializing a destroyed view silently did nothing.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
VERIFICATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Every file syntax checked. Beyond that, three suites were run against a real DOM:

  - Component, 30 assertions: all six bindings on first render and on patch,
    formatters, update() returning only the fields that moved, the data getter
    being a copy, absent values removing attributes, keyed sync building/patching/
    dropping and preserving node identity across a reorder, nested ownership and
    cascading destroy, both template forms, and nine validation failures each
    checked for the specific message.

  - DataView, 27 assertions, written as a regression check that the refactor
    changed nothing: rendering, node reuse across a patch, key drop/add, updateRow
    merging, sort moving the same nodes, filter, getData copying, config.data set
    before initialize, every pre-existing error string, and destroy/re-initialize.

  - The example page itself, 32 assertions: loaded into a DOM, its main.js run, then
    every control on the page driven and the result checked — including that all
    eleven failure-mode buttons raise a real Error.

All pass. The guide's error list was captured from the running module rather than
written by hand, and matches character for character.
